WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.770
Our weather club's chart

2
00:00:01.770 --> 00:00:03.450
showed a flood on Tuesday.

3
00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:06.580
It was the sunniest day of the week!

4
00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:09.700
One gauge had sent nine nine nine:

5
00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:11.840
its code for “I'm broken”.

6
00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:16.180
Today: blanks, repeats and impossible

7
00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:18.140
values, and how to catch them.

8
00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:20.920
Some values are impossible.

9
00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:24.340
A mark of twenty-five out of twenty.

10
00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:27.060
Rain of nine hundred and

11
00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.320
ninety-nine millimetres in a day.

12
00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:31.500
Minus four pupils.

13
00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:34.520
They need checking, not averaging.

14
00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:37.730
A blank is not a zero.

15
00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:39.700
Zero means we measured,

16
00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:40.840
and there was none.

17
00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:43.230
Blank means nobody measured.

18
00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:45.750
If you turn blanks into zeros,

19
00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:48.110
your average becomes a lie.

20
00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:51.450
Repeats are the same thing counted

21
00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:54.250
twice: one reading, sent two times.

22
00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:57.770
Remove the copy, but only when it's really

23
00:00:57.770 --> 00:01:00.110
the same station, on the same day.

24
00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:01.880
Quick check!

25
00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:05.350
A rain gauge reading is blank.

26
00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:08.530
What should you write in your report?

27
00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:11.530
That one reading is missing.

28
00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:14.460
Zero would be a fact you don't have,

29
00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:16.340
and the average would be a guess.

30
00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:19.450
Say what's missing, and find out why.

31
00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:22.750
Python can check every value.

32
00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:24.550
Loop through the marks,

33
00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:26.130
and count the blanks.

34
00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:28.540
Then flag any mark below

35
00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:30.520
zero or above twenty.

36
00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:34.370
A check is a rule the data must pass.

37
00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:36.940
In range: zero to twenty.

38
00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:38.190
Not empty.

39
00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:39.610
No repeats.

40
00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:42.690
When a check fails, set the row

41
00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:45.150
aside and ask; don't delete it.

42
00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:47.870
In the app, four weather

43
00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:49.310
clubs send rain readings.

44
00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:52.310
Turn on the checks one by one, and

45
00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:54.250
watch the rainfall report change.

46
00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:56.850
Turn them all off: suddenly

47
00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:58.770
there are six stations!

48
00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:00.700
Your turn.

49
00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:03.790
A mark says twenty-five, but the

50
00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:05.010
test was out of twenty.

51
00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:06.300
What do you do?

52
00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:08.400
Check it with the teacher:

53
00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:10.860
twenty-five out of twenty can't happen.

54
00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:14.410
Remember: a blank is not a zero.

55
00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:17.920
Look for repeats and impossible values.

56
00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:19.760
And when something's

57
00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:22.000
wrong, ask; don't guess.

58
00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:25.440
Try it tomorrow: check a table

59
00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:26.960
you made, or one from class.

60
00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:30.440
Circle every blank, every repeat

61
00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:32.840
and every impossible value.

62
00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:35.640
For each one, write what you'd do.
